# macOS
.DS_Store

# Editors
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Python (in case helper scripts get added)
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/

# Node (in case helper scripts get added)
node_modules/

# Local-only tool data
.cache/
.tmp/
*.log

# Local Pages build output (GitHub Action rebuilds from source)
site/_site/

# Sub-agent per-run artefact dir — v2.59 makes this versioned (the routine
# commits `work/<run-id>/` alongside the run's entries in Phase 6, so sub-agent
# findings, verification reports, url-liveness ledger, and per-agent
# timestamp checkpoints are auditable in git history). Operators who run
# the routine locally without committing should `git clean -fdx work/`
# between runs to avoid accumulating stale dirs.
#
# Editor / OS chaff inside the dir is still ignored:
work/**/.DS_Store
work/**/*.swp
work/**/*.bak

# Local Claude / preview tooling
.claude/launch.json

# Claude Code worktrees (created on demand by `/worktree` and similar; never commit)
.claude/worktrees/

# Never commit secrets
.env
.env.*
*secret*
*credential*

# ...but the secret-blocking globs above are filename-substring matches, and the
# content store legitimately produces entry / run / work filenames containing
# words like "credential" or "secret" (e.g. an entry on a hardcoded-credential
# CVE, or a credential-stuffing campaign). Those are published intelligence, not
# secrets. Without these negations the run silently commits fewer entries than it
# wrote — tools/check_run.py reads the filesystem, not the git index, so the gate
# passes and the missing entries only surface as a gap on the live site.
# Discovered 2026-07-30, when *credential* excluded two of that run's eight entries.
!entries/**
!runs/**
!work/**
